  "id": "COMPOUND:24595 - PROTEIN:6324",
  "verbalization": "Benzoylgomisin U is consistently identified as a target of multiple compounds, all of which ultimately lead to M-phase phosphoprotein 8. This suggests a shared mechanism of action involving the regulation of the cell cycle, particularly at the M-phase. The evidence points to a pathway where Benzoylgomisin U interacts with a common target, MIP, which is then further targeted by various compounds, including Quercetagetin 3-methyl ether 7-glucoside. These compounds are all implicated in modulating M-phase phosphoprotein 8, indicating a potential role in cell cycle regulation. The presence of multiple distinct molecular structures that share the same target, MIP, supports the idea that Benzoylgomisin U may act through a common mechanism, possibly involving the inhibition of a specific enzyme or protein involved in the M-phase. The integration of these findings suggests that Benzoylgomisin U may exert its effects by targeting a shared molecular pathway that influences the cell cycle, particularly at the M-phase, thereby contributing to the regulation of cell division and proliferation.",
